WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 154: Benjamin Harrison and the Pan-American Dream: The Forgotten Visionary (1889–1893)

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.760
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:06.960
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:18.560
[Rebecca] Today, we conclude our portrait of Benjamin Harrison by exploring his bold, visionary foreign policy and the deeply moving personal tragedy that defined the final months of his presidency.

4
00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:25.680
[Rebecca] Long before the 20th century, Harrison understood that America was destined to be a Pacific and hemispheric power.

5
00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:30.720
[Rebecca] Here is George with the full story.

6
00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:54.720
[George] Blaine, the aging plumed knight, was an imaginative, sweeping romantic who dreamed of binding the Western Hemisphere together under American diplomatic leadership.

7
00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:07.040
[George] Harrison, the precise constitutional lawyer and veteran general, provided the disciplined strategic focus and executive backbone necessary to turn Blaine's grand dreams into tangible statecraft.

8
00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:22.800
[George] For decades, European colonial powers, particularly Great Britain, France, and Germany, had dominated the trade and financial markets of Latin America, extracting raw materials and treating the Caribbean and South America as economic colonies.

9
00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:40.000
[George] Harrison and Blaine believed that the Monroe Doctrine should not be merely a passive defensive warning against European military conquest, but an active, positive alliance of independent American republics united by commerce, friendship, and compulsory international arbitration.

10
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:41.840
[George] Backslash N Backslash N.

11
00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:50.800
[George] Harrison and Blaine shared a profound belief that the United States was destined to become the commercial and diplomatic leader of the entire Western Hemisphere.

12
00:01:50.800 --> 00:02:01.160
[George] They envisioned a community of democratic nations tied together by mutual respect, free commerce, and legal arbitration, rather than European imperial coercion.

13
00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:13.880
[George] In October 1889, delegates from 17 Latin American nations arrived in Washington for the first International Conference of American States, the first Pan-American conference in history.

14
00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:22.440
[George] Harrison understood the importance of showing, rather than merely telling, these foreign diplomats the industrial power of the United States.

15
00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:33.800
[George] Before the conference began, Harrison placed the delegates aboard a luxurious private train for an unprecedented six-week, 6,000-mile tour of the nation's industrial heartland.

16
00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:46.040
[George] The Latin American delegates were shown the roaring blast furnaces of Pittsburgh, the grain elevators of Chicago, the automobile and carriage works of Detroit, and the textile mills of New England.

17
00:02:46.040 --> 00:03:02.680
[George] When the formal sessions opened in Washington, Harrison and Blaine proposed a comprehensive hemispheric customs union, a unified intercontinental railway, standard patent protections, and an arbitration treaty to peacefully resolve territorial disputes without bloodshed.

18
00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:18.760
[George] The conference established the Commercial Bureau of the American Republics, the direct ancestor of the modern Organization of American States, OAS, marking the birth of institutional pan-Americanism and establishing Washington as the diplomatic capital of the Western Hemisphere.

19
00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:32.280
[George] Backslash N Backslash N the Pan-American Tour was a diplomatic triumph, allowing Latin American envoys to witness firsthand the colossal manufacturing capacity and technological innovation of the United States.

20
00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:43.480
[George] Harrison personally hosted the visiting delegates at the White House, delivering speeches in which he emphasized that true hemispheric unity must be based on equality among sovereign nations.

21
00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:51.840
[George] The ultimate test of Harrison's diplomatic resolve erupted in October 1891 in the Pacific port of Valparaiso, Chile.

22
00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:58.160
[George] The United States cruiser USS Baltimore was anchored in the harbor during a violent Chilean civil war.

23
00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:07.600
[George] Captain Winfield Scott Schley granted shore leave to 117 unarmed American sailors, who gathered at the true blue saloon near the docks.

24
00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:14.080
[George] A drunken dispute between an American sailor and a Chilean local quickly escalated into a full-scale riot.

25
00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:25.200
[George] An enraged mob of several hundred Chilean civilians and armed municipal police surrounded the unarmed American sailors, attacking them with knives, clubs, and revolvers.

26
00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:33.840
[George] Two American sailors, Charles Riggan and William Turnbull, were brutally murdered, and seventeen others were severely stabbed and beaten.

27
00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:41.520
[George] Chilean police arrested 36 American sailors, dragging them through the streets and refusing medical treatment for their wounds.

28
00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:44.720
[George] Back in Washington, Harrison was incensed.

29
00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:51.520
[George] He viewed the unprovoked assault on uniformed American servicemen as an intolerable insult to the national honor.

30
00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:58.240
[George] Backslash N backslash N the assault on American sailors in Valparaiso threatened to spark a naval war.

31
00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:03.920
[George] But Harrison remained determined to defend the dignity of the United States flag at all costs.

32
00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:13.520
[George] He made it clear to European powers and Latin American governments alike that the United States would no longer tolerate the violent mistreatment of its uniformed personnel.

33
00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:19.200
[George] The newly installed revolutionary junta in Santiago reacted with arrogant defiance.

34
00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:32.920
[George] The Chilean foreign minister, Manuel Antonio Matta, dispatched an insulting diplomatic circular to European capitals, calling President Harrison a liar and accusing the American government of attempting to bully Chile.

35
00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:40.280
[George] Matta believed that Chile's modern, British-built ironclad navy could outmatch the antiquated American Pacific Fleet.

36
00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:48.600
[George] Secretary of State Blaine, who was ill, urged caution and appeasement, but Benjamin Harrison took personal charge of the crisis.

37
00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:53.800
[George] Calling in naval commanders, Harrison ordered the American fleet readied for war.

38
00:05:53.800 --> 00:06:12.760
[George] He dispatched modern steel cruisers to the Pacific and drafted an ultimatum to the Chilean government demanding three non-negotiable terms, a formal retraction of the insulting Mata circular, an official diplomatic apology to the United States, and a financial indemnity paid to the families of the murdered sailors.

39
00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:21.240
[George] On January 25, 1892, Harrison sent a special message to Congress, accompanied by naval battle plans.

40
00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:29.080
[George] Recognizing that Harrison was fully prepared to blockade Chilean ports and sink their navy, the Chilean government blinked.

41
00:06:29.080 --> 00:06:41.000
[George] In a complete capitulation, the junta withdrew the offensive circular, issued a humble public apology, and paid $75,000 in gold restitution to the families of the victims.

42
00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:48.120
[George] Harrison had defended American honor and demonstrated that the United States would protect its citizens anywhere in the world.

43
00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:49.960
[George] Backslash N backslash N.

44
00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:59.240
[George] Harrison's firm stand compelled the Chilean junta to back down, establishing that the United States would protect its citizens and servicemen across the globe.

45
00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:09.640
[George] The peaceful resolution of the crisis elevated American prestige throughout South America and proved that firmness, coupled with international law, could preserve the peace.

46
00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:17.600
[George] Yet just as Harrison achieved his greatest foreign policy triumphs, a devastating personal tragedy descended upon his home.

47
00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:32.000
[George] His beloved wife Caroline Scott Harrison, a talented musician, artist, and the founding president general of the Daughters of the American Revolution, fell gravely ill in late 1891 with pulmonary tuberculosis.

48
00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:40.160
[George] Caroline had been Benjamin's emotional anchor for nearly forty years, the warm, joyful counterweight to his reserved public demeanor.

49
00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:50.000
[George] As Caroline's health withered away in the sweltering Washington summer, Harrison moved her to Loon Lake in the Adirondack Mountains, hoping the mountain air would restore her lungs.

50
00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:52.240
[George] But the disease was relentless.

51
00:07:52.240 --> 00:08:02.880
[George] Harrison spent every moment he could away from his desk at his wife's bedside, holding her hand, reading aloud to her from scripture, and gently bathing her fevered brow.

52
00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:14.160
[George] When Caroline pleaded to return home to die in the White House, Harrison brought her back to the executive bedroom, cancelling all public receptions and spending long nights watching her breathe.

53
00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:21.760
[George] On October 25, 1892, just two weeks before the presidential election, Caroline passed away.

54
00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:33.680
[George] Harrison was utterly crushed with grief, weeping openly before his staff, backslash and backslash and Caroline Harrison's grace and dedication to historic preservation transformed the White House.

55
00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:47.520
[George] Establishing the first permanent collection of historic presidential China and supervising the complete wiring of the executive mansion for electric lights, her loss left a void in Benjamin Harrison's heart that never truly healed.

56
00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:55.680
[George] The presidential election of 1892 featured a historic rematch between Benjamin Harrison and former President Grover Cleveland.

57
00:08:55.680 --> 00:09:03.160
[George] Yet it was unlike any presidential campaign in American history, out of deep personal respect for the grieving president.

58
00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:10.200
[George] And because First Lady Caroline Harrison lay dying in the White House, Grover Cleveland took an extraordinary step.

59
00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:17.320
[George] He refused to actively campaign, deliver partisan speeches, or allow his supporters to hold raucous parades.

60
00:09:17.320 --> 00:09:23.080
[George] Harrison likewise remained at the White House in deep mourning, refusing to electioneer.

61
00:09:23.080 --> 00:09:31.560
[George] The campaign was conducted with solemn, dignified restraint on both sides, but the political headwinds facing Harrison were insurmountable.

62
00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:44.360
[George] The high prices of the McKinley tariff, coupled with violent labor unrest at Andrew Carnegie's homestead steel mills in Pennsylvania, turned working-class voters decisively against the Republican administration.

63
00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:56.840
[George] On November 8, 1892, Grover Cleveland scored an overwhelming victory, carrying the Electoral College 277 to 145 and sweeping the popular vote.

64
00:09:56.840 --> 00:10:00.600
[George] Benjamin Harrison accepted his defeat with serene relief.

65
00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:03.080
[George] He had fulfilled his duty to his country.

66
00:10:03.080 --> 00:10:07.080
[George] And his only desire was to return home to Indiana and rest.

67
00:10:07.080 --> 00:10:18.760
[George] Backslash and backslash end the campaign of 1892 was conducted with extraordinary dignity and restraint, reflecting the profound personal respect between Harrison and Grover Cleveland.

68
00:10:18.760 --> 00:10:28.200
[George] Both men refused to engage in the noisy partisan spectacles of the Gilded Age, setting a standard of decorum that was widely admired across the country.

69
00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:37.800
[George] Retiring to his elegant brick Victorian home on Delaware Street in Indianapolis, Benjamin Harrison enjoyed a magnificent Indian summer of public life.

70
00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:53.280
[George] He resumed his prestigious legal career, representing the Republic of Venezuela in a famous international boundary arbitration against Great Britain in Paris, delivering a brilliant, masterly 25-hour oral argument that earned him international acclaim.

71
00:10:53.280 --> 00:11:03.600
[George] He wrote a best-selling book on American statecraft titled This Country of Ours and delivered widely praised lectures at Stanford University on Constitutional Law.

72
00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:10.880
[George] In 1901, at age 67, Harrison developed a severe case of influenza that turned into pneumonia.

73
00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:19.040
[George] Surrounded by his family in Indianapolis, the former president passed away peacefully on March 13, 1901.

74
00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:27.360
[George] History has often overlooked Benjamin Harrison, remembering only his reserved demeanor and the single term between Cleveland's presidencies.

75
00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:43.520
[George] Yet Harrison left behind a remarkable legacy the birth of the modern Steel Navy, the creation of the National Forest System, the Sherman Antitrust Act, the expansion of six new Western states, and the visionary foundation of Pan American diplomacy.

76
00:11:43.520 --> 00:11:49.680
[George] He was an incorruptible patriot who served the Republic with towering intellect and uncompromising honor.

77
00:11:49.680 --> 00:12:01.680
[George] Backslash N backslash N in retirement, Harrison's brilliant oral argument in the Venezuelan boundary arbitration in Paris cemented his global reputation as a premier jurist and statesman.

78
00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:12.960
[George] His 25-hour address before the International Tribunal was hailed in London and Paris as one of the finest expositions of international law ever delivered by an American advocate.

79
00:12:13.520 --> 00:12:22.080
[Rebecca] Benjamin Harrison's vision of a strong, principled American presence in the Western Hemisphere laid the groundwork for the 20th century world order.

80
00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:27.600
[Rebecca] His intellect and legislative accomplishments remain a lasting model of executive stewardship.

81
00:12:27.600 --> 00:12:33.080
[Rebecca] Next week, we return to Grover Cleveland for his historic, crisis ridden second term.

82
00:12:33.080 --> 00:12:40.760
[Rebecca] The Panic of 1893, the Pullman Strike, and the top secret cancer surgery aboard a yacht in Long Island Sound.

83
00:12:40.760 --> 00:12:48.440
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

